(Plan 310 task 3 - a thing is a value, so assignment copies the whole thing
 and the copy shares nothing with the original (§5). A thing's size is a
 compile-time constant, so a copy is a run of inline moves: no allocation,
 no pointer, and nothing left aliased afterwards.

 The point pair proves the plainest copy is independent. The letter proves a
 copy is deep by construction - a nested thing is just more bytes, so
 copying the letter carries its point along and neither half is shared.
 Reading a whole nested thing is what makes 'the far corner' a copy of the
 reply's point rather than a second name for it.

 The last three spellings are the same statement in different words: a
 declaration with an initialiser, a bare `is`, and `Set ... to`. All three
 are assignment, so all three copy.

 Acceptance: the ten lines in the .expected, in that order.)

A thing called point has
  a number called x is 0,
  a number called y is 0.

A thing called letter has
  a point called postbox,
  a number called 'weight in grams' is 2.

a point called origin.
Set origin's x to 5.
a point called moved is origin.
Set moved's x to 9.
Print origin's x.
Print moved's x.

a letter called invitation.
Set invitation's postbox's x to 3.
a letter called reply is invitation.
Set reply's postbox's x to 7.
Print invitation's postbox's x.
Print reply's postbox's x.

a point called 'the far corner' is reply's postbox.
Set 'the far corner''s y to 1.
Print 'the far corner''s x.
Print reply's postbox's y.

Set reply's postbox to origin.
Print reply's postbox's x.
Print origin's x.

a point called elsewhere.
elsewhere is moved.
Print elsewhere's x.
Set elsewhere to origin.
Print elsewhere's x.
